{
 "id": "2022-lippert-urban",
 "name": "Urban Forest Types in Berlin: Attitudes and Emotions",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Lippert, Kowarik & Straka (2022), Land 11:701. Participants see four photographs of urban forest types in the Berlin region, one per \"four natures\" type: (1) a native beech remnant, (2) a pine plantation, (3) a park with non-native Robinia, and (4) a novel wild woodland with the non-native tree of heaven (Ailanthus). Each photo comes with the paper's description text. For each type they rate three attitude items (beautiful, worthy of protection, interesting) and four bipolar emotion pairs (negative–positive, dislike–like, uncomfortable–comfortable, unpleasant–pleasant), all on a −2..2 scale. They then answer three place-attachment items about Berlin and give demographics. Analysis as in the paper: mean attitude and emotion scores per type, Kruskal–Wallis tests with Bonferroni-corrected pairwise Wilcoxon tests, and GLMs on age, gender, place of growing up and place attachment. Stimuli: the four photographs of the paper's Figure 1 (photos by I. Kowarik, edited by the authors; CC BY 4.0), with the a–d panel labels cropped off. The leaf inserts are part of the stimuli and are kept. Credit: doi:10.3390/land11050701. The template is removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) The survey is in English; the original was in German and the wording follows the authors' translation in Table A1. (2) Attitude items are shown before emotion pairs, as described in Section 3.3.3; Table A1 lists the emotions first. (3) Questions on parents' and grandparents' countries of birth and on school and vocational education are omitted because they are not analysed in the paper. Country of birth and home language keep a single \"other\" option with no free text. (4) The other questionnaire items that the authors measured but did not report are not included. (5) The photos are slightly cropped at the bottom to remove the panel labels.",
